Linearity of $cal(D)$ splits the solution of $cal(D)(y) = f(x)$ into two parts:

1. Find the complementary functions $y_c$ satisfying the homogeneous equation $cal(D)(y_c) = 0$.
2. Find a particular integral $y_p$ satisfying $cal(D)(y_p) = f(x)$.
3. Combine them: since $cal(D)(y_c + y_p) = cal(D)(y_c) + cal(D)(y_p) = f(x)$, the sum $y = y_c + y_p$ solves the full equation.

A 2nd order ODE has two linearly independent complementary functions, so the general solution of the full equation is

$ y(x) = C_1 y_(c_1)(x) + C_2 y_(c_2)(x) + y_p (x). $
